  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...System bringing rain showers and chance for \nthunder moves in Sunday afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system in Canada moves eastward this weekend, with\ntrough axis moving through Sunday afternoon. Cold front moves \nthrough the state Sunday evening, funneling in some cooler air \nbehind it. Rain moves into the north, generally expanding \nsoutheastward during the day Sunday. PWATs generally around \n1-1.50 inches based on global and regional model outputs. Global\nmodels are keeping precipitation chances pretty modest, around \n14 of an inch, while regional models are hinting at some more \nconvective storms developing Sunday evening, bumping up rain \ntotals to one half to three quarters of an inch in isolated \nlocations, predominantly in the north and southeast. Instability\nparameters include few hundred joules of CAPE, 40-50 kts of \neffective shear, and some decent low level lapse rates, \nindicating that a thunderstorm is not out of the question. \nHeating ahead of the cold front in eastern Maine could aid in \ninitiating some convection along the boundary. Confidence is \nstill low for any severe thunderstorms to develop, given the \nmodel disagreement, but regional models are hinting at better \ninstability parameters than earlier indicated.",
